demos - programs to look at
===========================

These are GEM applications, cross compiled for the emulated machine with the
m68k-atari-mint tools, in the ordinary way an application is written. Nothing
in them knows it is not running on an ST.

They are here to be looked at rather than checked. The tests in tests/ say
whether something is right; these say what it looks like, which is a question
only a person can answer.

    make demos
    ./bin/tosemu build/demos/dialog

`TOSEMU_SCALE` says how much larger than an ST pixel one on the desktop is,
and three is the default.

With a compositor there is a window and the mouse and keyboard work. Without
one the screen is only in memory, which is not very interesting, though
TOSEMU_SCREENSHOT will still write out what was drawn:

    TOSEMU_NO_WINDOW=1 TOSEMU_SCREENSHOT=/tmp/shot.ppm ./bin/tosemu build/demos/dialog

and TOSEMU_KEYS and TOSEMU_CLICKS will press something, for when there is
nobody to. Keys are characters, with \r for Return. Clicks are places to press
and release the left button, and can also drag or only move:

    x,y             press and release there
    x,y-x2,y2       press at the first, release at the second
    @x,y            move the pointer there, pressing nothing

    TOSEMU_KEYS='\r' ./bin/tosemu build/demos/dialog
    TOSEMU_CLICKS='112,114' ./bin/tosemu build/demos/dialog
    TOSEMU_CLICKS='@96,5 @100,27 100,27' ./bin/tosemu build/demos/menu

Moving without pressing is not a nicety: a GEM menu opens when the pointer
arrives among the titles, so a menu cannot be worked by clicking alone.


The emulated screen is never shown. It is a coordinate space and a piece of
memory: GEM lays its windows out in it and draws into it the way it always did,
and an application cannot tell the difference. What appears on the desktop are
the windows themselves - one for each window GEM opens, one for each dialog -
with the desktop's own background showing through where an ST would have had
grey with an Atari logo on it.


window
------

A window with a title, a close box, a full box, an information line, a size box
and a line of text, and the loop every GEM application has: wait for a message,
redraw when told to, and stop when the close box is used.

The frame is GEM's own and the desktop puts nothing round the outside, so what
is there to click on is what the application asked for. Every one of those
gadgets does what its equivalent on this desktop does:

    the title bar    drag it to move the window; the other mouse button
                     brings up the desktop's own window menu, which is where
                     minimising lives - GEM has no gadget for it, a window
                     having had nowhere to go
    the close box    asks the application to close. It does not close anything
                     by itself: it sends the message GEM sent, and the
                     application decides, which is why one can ask whether you
                     meant it
    the full box     as large as the screen the AES lays windows out on, and
                     back again. That is not the same as filling the display
                     unless TOSEMU_SCREEN asked for a screen that size
    the size box     runs the desktop's own resize drag

The strip under the title bar is the information line, which is the one part of
the frame the AES neither reads nor acts on: the application sets the words and
the AES puts them there. This one says how large its work area is, so a drag on
the size box is readable from the inside.

`TOSEMU_DECORATIONS=desktop` puts it back the other way round, with the
desktop's frame and GEM's title bar left out. The information line stays either
way - nothing a desktop puts round a window says what the application wanted
said.

Resizing works the way closing does. Take hold of the size box and the desktop
runs the drag - with the outline and the edge snapping it would give any of its
own windows - and what the application gets when the drag ends is WM_SIZED, the
message GEM sent when somebody pulled its size box about. The window becomes
that size when the application sets its rectangle to what the message said; one
that ignored the message would keep the window it had.

While the drag is running the window is the size the drag has reached and what
is in it is a rubber band: an outline of alternate black and white pixels with
the desktop showing through the middle, which is what an ST drew while a window
was being sized. The application hears nothing until the button comes up and
then redraws once. Redrawing a document on every frame of a drag is slow enough
to feel on a 68000, and it is more than any application built for GEM was ever
asked to do - so the feedback is the emulator's to give and the drawing is the
application's, which is the division the rubber band was invented for.

A window can only be made as large as the screen the AES lays it out on, which
is 640x400 unless TOSEMU_SCREEN says otherwise - try `native-mono` for one as
large as the display. Grown against the bottom or the right of that screen it
is moved back rather than cut short, which is why WM_SIZED carries where as
well as how large.


dialog
------

A box, a question and two buttons.

form_dial reserves the screen the dialog sits on, which is what gives it a
window of its own: the desktop treats it as a dialog rather than as a picture
of one, so it is kept above the screen window, the screen window is out of
reach while it is up, and it can be dragged about with whatever the desktop
uses for moving windows - even though the program is blocked inside form_do
waiting for a button to be pressed.

Then objc_draw puts the tree up and form_do runs it: it waits, works out what
was clicked or typed, redraws the button that was pressed, and answers with
which object ended it.

The dialog is a window of the desktop's, so it has a frame and can be dragged
about even while the program is blocked waiting inside form_do. Its close
button sends Undo, the key GEM programs use for cancelling; form_do does not
listen for that one, so on this dialog it does nothing. GEM has no way of
saying a dialog was closed, and the only key that would end it is Return, which
presses the default button - closing a window should not be a way to agree to
something.

Click OK or Cancel, or press Return for OK - it is the default button, which
is why the AES draws it with a heavier border. Either way the program prints
which one it was and exits.

The tree is built in C rather than loaded from a resource file. A real
application would carry a .RSC and call rsrc_load, which needs a TOS path
resolved the way GEMDOS resolves one, and that is not written yet.


userdef
-------

The same dialog with the buttons drawn round instead of square, because the
program draws them itself.

Everything in a GEM dialog is one of a dozen kinds the AES knows how to draw,
except one: a G_USERDEF object - a G_PROGDEF to a resource editor - says "call
me and I will draw it", and what it points at is a routine in the application.
It is how every GEM program that did not look like a GEM program was written.

Here the AES and the application are not the same kind of code. The AES is the
emulator, running on the host; the routine is 68000 code in the emulated
machine. So this is the one place where a call goes the other way: the emulator
is halfway through drawing a dialog when it reaches an object it has to ask the
application about, and it starts the CPU again and runs it to the end of the
routine before carrying on.

The routine gets everything it needs in a block the AES builds in the machine's
own memory: which tree and which object, where it is, what it is clipped to,
and the state it is going into. The tree in that block is the application's own
rather than the copy the emulator has been drawing out of - this one reads the
object's flags out of it to find out whether it is the default button, and the
only tree it can read is its own.

    ./bin/tosemu build/demos/userdef

Hold a button down to see the routine draw the pressed state, which is its own
doing rather than the AES inverting the rectangle: an object that draws itself
is asked again when its state changes, and told what the state was as well as
what it is becoming.

The one rule is that it may only call the VDI. Calling the AES back would ask
it to start again on a tree it has not finished, so a call made from inside one
of these is refused and says so.


menu
----

A menu bar with two titles, and the shape a menu tree has to have.

Unlike a dialog, a menu is not a tree of any shape the application likes. The
AES walks it by position - the screen, the bar and the row of titles first, and
the box holding the drop-down menus last - and it rebuilds the Desk menu every
time a bar goes up, splicing in an entry for each accessory that has
registered. Those entries are written after the Desk menu's first one, so the
room for them has to be there whether or not anything ever fills it in. A
resource editor produces all of that without being asked, which is why it is so
rarely written down; this one builds it by hand, so it says it out loud.

The menu opens on hover rather than on a click, which is how GEM menus always
worked: the pointer arriving among the titles is what starts one, and the click
that follows is what chooses. Open File and choose Quit, or let go outside and
nothing is chosen.

The bar is a window of the desktop's like any other here, so it follows this
program about rather than sitting at the top of a picture of an ST.


fsel
----

The file selector, which is how a GEM application opens a file.

One call does all of it. The AES puts up the box, lists the directory, lets you
walk into folders and change drives, and answers with a path and a name. The
application draws none of it and reads no directories: it hands over two
buffers and reads them afterwards.

Those buffers are why this shows the selector twice. The path goes in as well
as coming out, so passing the same one again opens where the last one was left
- walk into a folder in the first box and the second starts there. That is the
whole of what "remembers the last directory" meant in 1988, and it is the
application remembering rather than the AES.

The second is fsel_exinput, the same thing with a line of the application's own
words along the top, so that a program asking where to save something can say
so instead of showing the box it showed for opening.

The box is a window of the desktop's like the dialogs are. It has to be asked
for separately from the way an application's own dialogs get one, because the
selector reserves its screen from inside the AES rather than by calling
form_dial - see the note in emuvdi/bridge.c.


acc
---

A desk accessory, built as DEMO.ACC.

What makes one an accessory is not what it is made of - it is a GEM application
like any other - but that it never stops. A program runs, does something and
exits; an accessory says what it is called and then waits for the rest of the
session to ask for it.

Saying what it is called is menu_register, and it is the only thing that makes
an accessory reachable: the name goes into the Desk menu of every application
running, including the ones that started first and the ones that have not
started yet. Picking it there sends the accessory AC_OPEN, which is its cue to
put a window up.

It answers AP_TERM by leaving, which is the one message an accessory does fall
out of its loop for: not an application closing, which happens all day, but the
thing that started it saying there will not be another. An accessory that
ignores it is stopped instead, which works and is less tidy.

It needs a daemon, because the menu it appears in belongs to the other
applications rather than to itself:

    make demos
    mkdir -p /tmp/acc && cp build/demos/DEMO.ACC /tmp/acc/
    bin/tosaesd -v /tmp/acc &
    bin/tosemu build/demos/menu

and the Desk menu has Demo in it, under a separator. Picking it prints a line
from the accessory rather than doing anything to the menu demo, which is the
whole point.
